{{also|Floss|FLOSS}}
==English==

===Etymology===
{{wikipedia|Dental floss}}
[[Image:Spinning candy floss.jpg|thumb|right|A woman spinning candy floss.]]

From {{etyl|fr|en}} {{term|floche||tuft of wool|lang=fr}}, from {{term|floc|lang=fr}}, from {{etyl|la|en}} {{term|floccus||piece of wool|lang=la}} < an unrecorded O.E. or O.N. word from the root of Dutch ''flos'' "plush" (17c.). Compare to modern German {{term|Fluss||a winding section/ a flowing river|lang=de}}. Also related to [[fleece]] and [[flow]].

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en-us-floss.ogg|Audio (US)}}
*: {{rhymes|ɒs}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|es}}

# a [[thread]], used to clean the area between the [[teeth]]
# (raw) [[silk]] fibres
# the fibres covering a [[corn]] [[cob]]
# Any thread-like material having parallel [[strands]] that are not [[spun]] or [[wound]] around each other.
#: ''embroidery '''floss'''''
# {{British}} Spun sugar or [[cotton candy]], especially in the phrase "candy floss".

